  [1mThe Quiet Wood[0m

  Beneath the oldest oaks the morning waits,
  and light falls through the leaves in scattered gold.
  Ferns open slowly, like a row of gates,
  and wind moves through the hush, a story told.

  Each trunk holds rings I never learn to count,
  a ledger written slowly, year by year.
  The fallen logs dissolve to soil and mount
  new seedlings, green and eager, far and near.

  A woodpecker taps out a patient beat
  against a trunk that hums with hidden life.
  A fox slips past on careful, silent feet,
